<!-- ============================== GLOSSARY ================================ -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <dl class="glossary">
        <dt>Biosignature</dt>
        <dd>A chemical or spectral feature best explained by life — typically an atmospheric disequilibrium nothing geological can maintain.</dd>
        <dt>Technosignature</dt>
        <dd>Evidence of technology rather than biology: a narrowband transmission, industrial pollutants, waste heat, a megastructure.</dd>
        <dt>Habitable zone</dt>
        <dd>The orbital band where liquid water can persist on a surface. Necessary, nowhere near sufficient.</dd>
        <dt>Great Filter</dt>
        <dd>Hanson's proposed improbable step between dead chemistry and a galactic civilisation. Whether it lies behind us or ahead of us is the whole question.</dd>
        <dt>Dark forest</dt>
        <dd>The hypothesis that broadcasting one's position is lethally unwise, so everyone sensible stays quiet.</dd>
        <dt>UAP</dt>
        <dd>Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena. The replacement term for UFO, adopted to escape the cultural baggage and to include non-flying cases.</dd>
        <dt>Pareidolia</dt>
        <dd>The perceptual tendency to impose familiar form — faces, craft, figures — on noise. A feature of a predictive brain, not a defect.</dd>
        <dt>Sleep paralysis</dt>
        <dd>REM muscle atonia persisting into wakefulness, with a hallucinated presence. The physiological core of many night encounters.</dd>
        <dt>Panspermia</dt>
        <dd>The proposal that life, or its precursors, travels between worlds on debris. Plausible for chemistry; unproven for organisms.</dd>
        <dt>Densities</dt>
        <dd>In the <em>Law of One</em>, graded octaves of conscious experience through which all things evolve. <span class="myth">◆</span></dd>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Reading</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Carl Sagan</strong>, <em>The Demon-Haunted World</em> (1995) — written by a man who spent his life searching for aliens, which is exactly what makes its chapter on abduction so bracing.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Nick Lane</strong>, <em>The Vital Question</em> (2015) — on why the jump to complex cells may be the rarest step of all.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Adam Frank</strong>, <em>The Little Book of Aliens</em> (2023) — current, honest, written by an astrophysicist who takes UAP seriously enough to assess them properly.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>ODNI</strong>, <em>Preliminary Assessment: Unidentified Aerial Phenomena</em> (2021) — nine pages, read the primary document.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Jacques Vallée</strong>, <em>Passport to Magonia</em> (1969) — the fairy-faith-to-flying-saucer continuity argument, and still the most interesting book in the field.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>D. W. Pasulka</strong>, <em>American Cosmic</em> (2019) — a religious-studies scholar on belief, technology and how a new religion forms in real time.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>Avi Loeb</strong>, <em>Extraterrestrial</em> (2021) — the 'Oumuamua argument from its most prominent advocate; read with the rebuttals beside it.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>Elkins, Rueckert &amp; McCarty</strong>, <em>The Law of One</em>, I–V (1984) — the primary channelled text, free in full online.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
